OCPROJECT 1
# Engine test content for the CONTROL RIG. Not a demo game -- it exists so a real .ocrig, authored
# against a real humanoid skeleton, sits somewhere the editor can open and a person can read.
#
# WHAT IS HERE, AND WHAT IS HONESTLY NOT.
#
# Here: Content/Rigs/ArmReach.ocrig, a two-op rig (a two-bone IK on the left arm and a weighted head
# aim) written against Content/Skeletons/Character.ocskel's actual bone names. Open the Content
# Browser on this project and it is there, with an icon; open the file and it reads as what it does.
# Every layer beneath it is built and tested -- anim::twoBoneIk and anim::aimAt (IkTest), the format
# (OcRigTest), and CControlRig applied through AnimSystem's pose-modifier seam (ControlRigTest,
# which drives exactly this chain end to end from a file on disk).
#
# NOT here: a MAP that places the character with this rig attached. That is not an oversight and it
# is not a missing file -- the level format cannot yet express it. An .ocworld/.ocmap PLACE record
# carries an asset, a transform, a material and a class name; it has no skeleton field, so a placed
# mesh is a static mesh, and a static mesh has no pose for a rig to modify. Skinned, animated
# characters reach a level through a graph CLASS instead (that is how templates/FirstPerson does it),
# and there is no Aver Node node that attaches a control rig yet.
#
# So the honest state is: the rig runs, and is proven to run, from code and from a test; it cannot
# yet be PLACED from a level file. Closing that needs one of two small things -- a `rig=` attribute
# on a placement plus skeleton support, or a SetControlRig node in the Aver Node palette -- and
# neither should be guessed at before somebody has authored a second rig.
NAME ControlRigDemo
ENGINE Aver 0.5.0
CREATEDWITH 0.5.0
CONTENT Content
